  #set text(font: ("Times New Roman", "New Computer Modern Math"), size: 11.019pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(90pt, 82.08pt, "which a continuous vapor layer separates the liquid droplet from the hot substrate. This vapor layer ")
  #line-at(90pt, 95.76pt, "acts as a thermal insulator, enabling a droplet lifetime of approximately 80 s (compared to less than ")
  #line-at(90pt, 109.44pt, "0.5 s at 150 °C). On the post substrate, however, the droplet remains attached to the post structure ")
  #line-at(90pt, 123pt, "and remains in partial contact with the horizontal base surface. Atomization is observed around the ")
  #line-at(90pt, 136.68pt, "contact line region, indicating strong vaporization at the base. The droplet shows a “candle” shape ")
  #line-at(90pt, 150.36pt, "without lifting off the post and completely vaporizes in around 0.6 s. A comparison of the detailed ")
  #line-at(90pt, 163.92pt, "droplet dynamics for both surfaces is provided in Supplementary Material Section 1.  ")
  #line-at(90pt, 185.64pt, "This interesting boiling behavior on the post substrate is attributed to a mixed boiling mode of the ")
  #line-at(90pt, 199.32pt, "droplet. On the base substrate surrounding the post, the temperature is higher than the Leidenfrost ")
  #line-at(90pt, 212.88pt, "temperature")
  #line-at(145.2pt, 212.88pt, "of the flat surface (160 ℃). The liquid-solid interface there is consequently in the film ")
  #line-at(90pt, 226.56pt, "boiling mode, in which the droplet detaches from the surface. However, since the post is entirely ")
  #line-at(90pt, 240.24pt, "engulfed by the droplet, its temperature is decreased by the surrounding water body, allowing for ")
  #line-at(90pt, 253.92pt, "nucleate boiling rather than film boiling towards its top. The temperature decrease along the post ")
  #line-at(90pt, 267.48pt, "can be estimated using an analytical model based on the heat conduction equation. Figure 3 (a) ")
  #line-at(90pt, 281.16pt, "shows the schematic of the boundary conditions. Several assumptions can be made to simplify the ")
  #line-at(90pt, 294.84pt, "problem. First, we assume a 1-dimensional (1D) temperature distribution along the vertical ")
  #line-at(90pt, 308.52pt, "direction due to the aluminum’s high thermal conductivity and the axisymmetric design. Second, ")
  #line-at(90pt, 322.08pt, "the sidewall and top surfaces experience (nucleate) boiling and have the same constant heat ")
  #line-at(90pt, 335.76pt, "convection boundary condition, which can be estimated using the lifetime, total energy (sensible ")
  #line-at(90pt, 349.44pt, "and latent heat), and contact area of the droplet. Detailed information on the estimation of the heat ")
  #line-at(90pt, 363.12pt, "transfer coefficient can be found in Supplementary Material Section 2. Third, an isothermal ")
  #line-at(90pt, 376.68pt, "boundary condition with the temperature equal to the initial steady-state substrate temperature is ")
  #line-at(90pt, 390.36pt, "assumed at the bottom of the post. Additionally, we assume temperature-independence of ")
