  #set text(font: ("Times New Roman", "New Computer Modern Math"), size: 9.941pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(90pt, 313.8pt, "Fig. S4 ")
  #set text(font: ("Times New Roman", "New Computer Modern Math"), size: 9.941pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(122.4pt, 313.8pt, "We")
  #set text(font: ("Times New Roman", "New Computer Modern Math"), size: 9.941pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(135.12pt, 313.8pt, "-")
  #set text(font: ("Times New Roman", "New Computer Modern Math"), size: 9.941pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(138.36pt, 313.8pt, "T")
  #set text(font: ("Times New Roman", "New Computer Modern Math"), size: 6.468pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(144pt, 314.76pt, "S")
  #set text(font: ("Times New Roman", "New Computer Modern Math"), size: 9.941pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(147.48pt, 313.8pt, " regime maps for droplet impact on (a) a flat substrate and (b) a post substrate. Six different ")
  #line-at(90pt, 325.32pt, "impact and boiling modes are observed. The orange dashed line is a visual guide for the transition between ")
  #line-at(90pt, 336.84pt, "droplet rebound and non-rebound regimes. The black dashed line represents the transition between rebound ")
  #line-at(90pt, 348.36pt, "and breakup regimes. ")
  #set text(font: ("Times New Roman", "New Computer Modern Math"), size: 15.93pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(90pt, 377.64pt, "5")
  #line-at(111.6pt, 377.64pt, "Effects of droplet size ")
  #set text(font: ("Times New Roman", "New Computer Modern Math"), size: 11.019pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(90pt, 392.64pt, "The experiment results that we present in this study are based on a droplet size of 2.7 mm. The ")
  #line-at(90pt, 406.32pt, "droplet-to-post ratio 2.7 is chosen based on several reasons. First, the droplet is expected to easily ")
  #line-at(90pt, 419.88pt, "cover the post structure and maintain an axisymmetric shape (not lean to one side), which requires ")
  #line-at(90pt, 433.56pt, "a ratio larger than 1.5. Second, the post structure should still be large enough to make significant ")
  #line-at(90pt, 447.24pt, "changes to the static droplet shape or dynamic behavior, which favors a ratio smaller than 3. Third, ")
  #line-at(90pt, 460.92pt, "for a ratio ranging from 1.5 to 3, we would like to maximize the droplet volume to acquire the ")
  #line-at(90pt, 474.48pt, "largest cooling capacity. Therefore, a droplet volume of 2.7 mm, which is easily generated by a 25-")
  #line-at(90pt, 488.04pt, "gauge needle, was chosen for the 1 mm post substrate.   ")
  #line-at(90pt, 509.88pt, "Here we used a smaller needle size to generate droplets at 1.8 ± 0.05 mm and selected representative ")
  #line-at(90pt, 523.44pt, "surface temperatures of 200 ℃ and 250 ℃ to investigate the droplet impact and boiling modes on ")
  #line-at(90pt, 537.12pt, "a post substrate (shown in Fig. S5). At 200 ℃, the smaller droplet already shows a rebound at We ")
  #line-at(90pt, 550.8pt, "= 23. However, in Fig 5, the original 2.7 mm-droplet rebounds at We = 31. This difference indicates ")
  #line-at(90pt, 564.48pt, "that the smaller droplet size provides an early rebound, which can be attributed to a smaller transient ")
  #line-at(90pt, 578.04pt, "cooling effect that increase the vapor thickness. For droplet breakup, we found that the breakup ")
  #line-at(90pt, 591.72pt, "transition Weber numbers are very similar between the 2.7 mm and 1.8 mm droplety. This result ")
  #line-at(90pt, 605.4pt, "indicates that the droplet breakup on the post substrate is still highly determined by the relative ")
  #line-at(90pt, 618.96pt, "strength between inertia and capillary forces.  ")
]

